00:00:40.578 --> 00:00:42.160
I love that y'all planned that obviously,

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:43.490
you were like, "Let's
show up and do something?"

00:00:43.490 --> 00:00:44.323
Like 'cause y'all were-

00:00:44.323 --> 00:00:45.350
- Yeah, well, we were asked

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:48.040
'cause they said the theme
was back together again.

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:50.017
So they asked Mira or I
to present and Mira said,

00:00:50.017 --> 00:00:52.060
"Hey in the script it says ensemble.

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:53.830
And so we should be wearing ensembles."

00:00:53.830 --> 00:00:56.010
And I went immediately to
what we wore in the movie.

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:58.107
I was like, ah, I don't
know if I can do that.

00:00:58.107 --> 00:01:00.190
And really sounds like a lot of work.

00:01:00.190 --> 00:01:03.770
And, then someone showed me a picture

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:06.130
of this pink sequin tuxedo.

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:07.500
I went, well, I can do that.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:08.910
And Mira can wear a blue suit.

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:09.743
- [Kelly] Oh my God.

00:01:09.743 --> 00:01:10.576
- And she did.

00:01:10.576 --> 00:01:11.409
- [Kelly] She did.

00:01:11.409 --> 00:01:12.242
You looked fantastic.

00:01:12.242 --> 00:01:13.203
It was amazing.

00:01:13.203 --> 00:01:14.036
- [Lisa] Oh thanks.

00:01:14.036 --> 00:01:14.869
Yeah.

00:01:14.869 --> 00:01:15.702
And it's awesome.

00:01:15.702 --> 00:01:16.680
- Cause people attach them, some

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:18.560
of these characters that y'all get to play

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:20.590
and we love and then
free all to like do that.

00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:21.423
It's really cool.

00:01:21.423 --> 00:01:22.680
It was nostalgic for everyone.

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:24.880
So, well, is it true

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:26.810
that you originally didn't
even want to do acting

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:27.760
that you wanted to be a doctor?

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:28.593
I didn't know this.

00:01:28.593 --> 00:01:29.947
- No, that is absolutely true.

00:01:29.947 --> 00:01:30.780
- I never heard about that.

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:31.613
Me too.

00:01:31.613 --> 00:01:33.100
I was, I was gonna be a doctor too.

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:33.933
- Really?

00:01:33.933 --> 00:01:34.766
- And then I was like

00:01:34.766 --> 00:01:35.686
I guess I'll see.

00:01:35.686 --> 00:01:36.519
(all laughing)

00:01:36.519 --> 00:01:37.358
Like that's amazing.

00:01:37.358 --> 00:01:38.360
(both chattering)

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:40.129
- Hey, that doesn't sound bad.

00:01:40.129 --> 00:01:40.962
- Wait. No, that's amazing.

00:01:40.962 --> 00:01:42.540
Not many people are like,
"I'm gonna be a doctor."

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:43.470
That's amazing.

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:45.420
- Yeah. Well my father's a doctor.

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:46.253
My brother was in medical school.

00:01:46.253 --> 00:01:48.430
- [Kelly] Oh, okay. So
you felt that pressure?

00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:49.310
- No. Not at all.

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:50.950
I found it fascinating.

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:52.320
I mean I loved biology.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:53.560
I took honors biology.

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:54.980
I was like, well this is it for me.

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:55.813
For sure.

00:01:55.813 --> 00:01:56.646
- [Kelly] Oh, that's cool.

00:01:56.646 --> 00:01:57.587
Yeah. Yeah, but and the I-

00:01:57.587 --> 00:01:59.579
- How did that shift happened, that shift.

00:01:59.579 --> 00:02:02.344
- Yeah. Well this is a
little weird, but so,

00:02:02.344 --> 00:02:03.460
(group laughing)

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:06.750
I mean, whenever I would come home

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:08.590
from like spring break or something

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:12.560
from college and I lived in
LA and I'd be driving around

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:15.110
and I'd hear a commercial
on the radio for a sitcom

00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:18.400
and they'd be, you know,
promoting their best joke.

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:21.560
And it just sounded like it
was being like punched so hard.

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:26.080
Like, well that's a different
thing and I was like, oh.

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:26.960
- That makes me cringe.

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:30.830
- And in my head I would think,
oh my God, don't punch it

00:02:30.830 --> 00:02:33.943
like that Lisa just remember
throw it away, throw it away.

00:02:33.943 --> 00:02:35.170
(crowd laughs)

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:36.003
- And I went

00:02:36.003 --> 00:02:36.836
- When you're in that position

00:02:36.836 --> 00:02:38.860
- And that was my first thought was like,

00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:39.700
yeah, throw it away.

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:43.780
Wait, what? why would I
need to remember that?

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:46.800
And those kinds of things
kept coming up and happening.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:47.680
Like David Letterman

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:49.850
and I'd see someone on I'm
like, oh, she seems so phony.

00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:51.682
Lisa, when you're on
Letterman, don't be phony.

00:02:51.682 --> 00:02:52.701
(audience laughing)

00:02:52.701 --> 00:02:55.323
And then I went wait,

00:02:55.323 --> 00:02:58.570
why would I ever be on David Letterman?

00:02:58.570 --> 00:03:00.023
That doesn't even make sense.

00:03:00.023 --> 00:03:00.970
- You're creating the
vision board in your head.

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:01.803
I love that.

00:03:01.803 --> 00:03:02.636
I just love the shift.

00:03:02.636 --> 00:03:04.460
So finally you leaned into
the voice in your head.

00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:06.331
I was like, don't do that
when you're on Letterman.

00:03:06.331 --> 00:03:07.164
- I did.

00:03:07.164 --> 00:03:09.220
I needed like a practical, rational thing.

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:10.180
Like you know what?

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:11.170
You don't have kids.

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:12.610
You don't have any responsibilities.

00:03:12.610 --> 00:03:14.130
You can go for this now.

00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:14.970
- I love that.

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:16.490
I'm so glad you did.

00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:17.323
Oh my God, So I.

00:03:17.323 --> 00:03:18.156
- I am too,

00:03:18.156 --> 00:03:19.077
Yeah. Yeah.

00:03:19.077 --> 00:03:20.473
- It was a life changing.

00:03:20.473 --> 00:03:22.620
So you probably let
your doctor family down

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:23.496
but that's okay.

00:03:23.496 --> 00:03:24.329
(people laughing)

00:03:24.329 --> 00:03:25.162
- They were thrilled.

00:03:25.162 --> 00:03:27.170
- I'm just kidding. I'm just kidding.

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:28.070
No, but your show, Who
Do You Think You Are?

00:03:28.070 --> 00:03:30.310
I was so excited to do it.

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:32.890
Not only me, but my mother was
very excited for me to do it.

00:03:32.890 --> 00:03:36.470
And when I did it, I just
had no idea cuz my family,

00:03:36.470 --> 00:03:37.800
I mean all of us,
especially in America world

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:38.633
wore muts, right?

00:03:38.633 --> 00:03:40.670
And there's so many different
layers to our background.

00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:41.503
- Yeah.

00:03:41.503 --> 00:03:42.800
- And I just had no idea what direction

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:44.300
and it was the coolest thing ever.

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:45.133
- Yeah.

00:03:45.133 --> 00:03:46.580
- To find out about Isaiah R. Rose.

00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:48.120
My great-great great, great grandfather.

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:49.560
It was like the coolest thing ever.

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:50.393
- [Lisa] Yeah.

00:03:50.393 --> 00:03:51.709
- What's the coolest
thing you've ever found

00:03:51.709 --> 00:03:52.810
out on that show, like?

00:03:52.810 --> 00:03:53.820
- The coolest thing?

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:55.190
- Yeah. Just something that sticks out.

00:03:55.190 --> 00:03:57.600
- I have to say the thing
that sticks out since

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:01.220
I started doing that show
was how much I was learning

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:02.430
about history.

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:03.263
- [Kelly] Oh.

00:04:03.263 --> 00:04:04.800
- That you don't learn in school.

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:05.633
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:05.633 --> 00:04:07.770
- That there are these personal stories

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:11.470
and experiences that do
not get taught at all.

00:04:11.470 --> 00:04:15.350
And just like the nuances
of history that are really

00:04:15.350 --> 00:04:18.300
important to understand the
context for people's lives.

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:19.133
And so it's great.

00:04:19.133 --> 00:04:20.070
And I'm glad you brought it

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:23.561
up because we are, there's
gonna be more on NBC.

00:04:23.561 --> 00:04:24.670
- Oh! On NBC?

00:04:24.670 --> 00:04:25.510
- [Lisa] In July.

00:04:25.510 --> 00:04:26.343
- Oh! That's amazing.

00:04:26.343 --> 00:04:28.062
- I know. I'm so happy. Yeah.

00:04:28.062 --> 00:04:29.232
- What can we expect?

00:04:29.232 --> 00:04:30.690
Can you talk about anyone yet?

00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:33.262
- Yeah. I can tell you
who's gonna be in it.

00:04:33.262 --> 00:04:35.740
We'll do six episodes.

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:40.563
So we'll have Allison Janney, Zach Quinto.

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:44.707
Oh, I knew that I would do this.

00:04:44.707 --> 00:04:45.737
(all laughing)

00:04:45.737 --> 00:04:46.570
Oh, Billy Porter.

00:04:46.570 --> 00:04:47.900
Yeah. Gonna be good.

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:49.150
Nick Offerman.

00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:50.210
- [Kelly] I love him.

00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:52.980
- Yes. Zach LaVine and Bradley Whitford.

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:54.020
- [Kelly] That's so cool.

00:04:54.020 --> 00:04:54.940
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:55.960
They're great.

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:57.083
They've unbelievable stories.

00:04:57.083 --> 00:04:57.916
- It's yeah.

00:04:57.916 --> 00:05:00.640
It's just really cool to
dive into that and I never

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:02.926
would've and I think just so you know,

00:05:02.926 --> 00:05:04.560
it's a cool experience for like,

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:05.980
I just grew up with my mom.

00:05:05.980 --> 00:05:07.610
- Like I didn't even grow up
with my brother or sister.

00:05:07.610 --> 00:05:09.550
Like I didn't grow up with
any family, like aunts, uncles

00:05:09.550 --> 00:05:10.510
grandparents, nothing.

00:05:10.510 --> 00:05:13.610
So like to go into that
show and like actually

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:16.040
find family is really
cool for a lot of people

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:17.750
that didn't have that like growing up.

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:19.463
You feel attached to
something it's beautiful.

00:05:19.463 --> 00:05:20.296
- Yes. Yes.

00:05:20.296 --> 00:05:21.129
You're connected.

00:05:21.129 --> 00:05:22.310
You're connected to your roots.

00:05:22.310 --> 00:05:24.890
And you're also connected to the history.

00:05:24.890 --> 00:05:25.723
Of this place.

00:05:25.723 --> 00:05:28.040
- And I've always felt like
a leaf, so not no roots.

00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:29.090
So it was like nice.

00:05:29.090 --> 00:05:29.923
- [Both] Yeah.

00:05:29.923 --> 00:05:31.687
I've never felt like a leaf.

00:05:31.687 --> 00:05:32.520
I don't know why I said that.

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:33.353
(audience applauding)

00:05:33.353 --> 00:05:34.233
That was the look of her new
movie "Better Nate Than Ever"

00:05:34.233 --> 00:05:36.580
"Better Nate Than Ever" Looks very fun.

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:37.675
Can you tell us about it?

00:05:37.675 --> 00:05:38.650
- It's really fun.

00:05:38.650 --> 00:05:39.658
"Better Nate Than Ever".

00:05:39.658 --> 00:05:40.491
And that kid that's Ruby.

00:05:40.491 --> 00:05:41.610
He's amazing.

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:44.270
Well, so that is, I was told, okay, please

00:05:44.270 --> 00:05:47.271
you've gotta do this because
this is for Disney Plus it's

00:05:47.271 --> 00:05:49.730
the first film where
the lead character is a

00:05:49.730 --> 00:05:52.030
13 year old boy who's gay.

00:05:52.030 --> 00:05:54.476
And I went, wow. Okay. That's cool.

00:05:54.476 --> 00:05:55.660
That's worth supporting.

00:05:55.660 --> 00:06:00.210
And then I read the script and
it's so fun and it's so good.

00:06:00.210 --> 00:06:04.590
And what I loved is that
this kid, Nate is different.

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:05.530
He's bullied.

00:06:05.530 --> 00:06:08.210
His brother doesn't know and
gets him, but his best friend.

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:10.740
And, you can tell he can't help it,

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:12.320
but he like, just likes himself.

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:13.153
He can't help it.

00:06:13.153 --> 00:06:13.986
But he just does.

00:06:13.986 --> 00:06:18.000
And every time he is just
himself, everything takes off.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:20.485
And so I loved that.

00:06:20.485 --> 00:06:21.497
- I love that concept.

00:06:21.497 --> 00:06:22.330
Yeah.

00:06:22.330 --> 00:06:23.196
- I loved that.

00:06:23.196 --> 00:06:24.560
- Cause I do think that's a good lesson.

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:26.010
Like when you do believe
in yourself the most

00:06:26.010 --> 00:06:27.240
then things will start to happen.

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:29.910
- Right. Or it's just, you're
allowed to like yourself.

00:06:29.910 --> 00:06:32.090
You know, I think we all
spend a lot of time and,

00:06:32.090 --> 00:06:36.354
especially when you're
like 12, 13, 14, and 50.

00:06:36.354 --> 00:06:38.156
(audience laughing)

00:06:38.156 --> 00:06:40.100
You know, trying to come up with reasons,

00:06:40.100 --> 00:06:41.420
why? Oh, I did that wrong.

00:06:41.420 --> 00:06:42.290
Oh, that's not good.

00:06:42.290 --> 00:06:43.303
Well, you suck at that.

00:06:43.303 --> 00:06:44.136
- Self-critical. Yeah.

00:06:44.136 --> 00:06:45.520
- And it's so unnecessary.

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:46.860
It's just a waste of time.

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:48.230
It's really not necessary.

00:06:48.230 --> 00:06:50.586
And, and it's really, it's just okay.

00:06:50.586 --> 00:06:52.666
Like yourself and you'll see.

00:06:52.666 --> 00:06:54.863
Things take off when you're you. You know?

00:06:54.863 --> 00:06:55.970
(audience cheering)

00:06:55.970 --> 00:06:57.223
- I agree.

00:06:59.337 --> 00:07:00.830
Well, wait, Phoebe
always played the guitar.

00:07:00.830 --> 00:07:02.870
So did you know how to play
guitar before the show?

00:07:02.870 --> 00:07:03.703
- No.

00:07:03.703 --> 00:07:04.536
- [Kelly] No?

00:07:04.536 --> 00:07:05.369
- No, but I thought,

00:07:05.369 --> 00:07:06.202
- Did they terrify you?

00:07:06.202 --> 00:07:07.035
- They wanted me to learn.

00:07:07.035 --> 00:07:08.690
No. Oh, they.

00:07:08.690 --> 00:07:10.007
- I love you.

00:07:10.007 --> 00:07:12.222
(both chattering)

00:07:12.222 --> 00:07:13.580
But you know, they wanted me to,

00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:15.080
they said, we'll give you guitar lessons.

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:18.470
I said fine, because my
family's really musical.

00:07:18.470 --> 00:07:20.430
My brother's a phenomenal guitar player,

00:07:20.430 --> 00:07:21.470
and all the way back.

00:07:21.470 --> 00:07:23.863
They're all musicians. It turns out

00:07:23.863 --> 00:07:24.930
- Musicians and doctors. Okay.

00:07:24.930 --> 00:07:26.310
- Except for me.

00:07:26.310 --> 00:07:29.270
So, you know, cuz I pick up
the guitar and take a lesson.

00:07:29.270 --> 00:07:30.990
So like, well this hurts my fingers.

00:07:30.990 --> 00:07:32.593
- Oh yeah. Yeah. You get calluses.

00:07:32.593 --> 00:07:33.584
- A lot.

00:07:33.584 --> 00:07:36.080
And boy, I don't know if I really feel it,

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:39.250
or want to, but I struggled.

00:07:39.250 --> 00:07:40.740
I kept with it, you know?

00:07:40.740 --> 00:07:43.690
And that first season or
second season, I don't know.

00:07:43.690 --> 00:07:45.210
Chrisy Hein was on.

00:07:45.210 --> 00:07:47.650
That's the first time
'smelly cat' was on the show.

00:07:47.650 --> 00:07:48.483
Right?

00:07:48.483 --> 00:07:49.685
- Yeah. And.

00:07:49.685 --> 00:07:52.852
(audience applauding)

00:07:53.795 --> 00:07:55.590
- And I was saying, yeah,
I'm trying to learn.

00:07:55.590 --> 00:07:57.630
She's like trying to learn
what you only need to

00:07:57.630 --> 00:07:59.010
know three chords.

